{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And he said to Balak's servants: If Balak would give me.</b> Above, he mentioned Balak's officers with reference to those who were lesser; but concerning these who were more numerous and distinguished, he said 'Balak's servants.' The explanation is that Balaam was haughty and arrogant of heart, and the first ones who came before him came with humility and spoke to him in soft language, so he called them 'officers'; but these who spoke to him with pride — 'Thus says Balak son of Zippor: Do not refrain, please, from going to me' — he called them 'servants,' to indicate that they were lowly in his eyes and he accounted them only as servants. And further, to indicate that just as they were servants of Balak, who was a king of flesh and blood, and are not permitted to transgress his command at all — how much more so is he not permitted to transgress the word of the Holy One, blessed be He. And this is what he said: 'If Balak would give me, etc.'"], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The abbreviation הש\"י is expanded as השם יתברך, rendered here as 'the Holy One, blessed be He' per the voice rules for reverent divine abbreviations."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:02.133462049Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}